The discriminants in source order are $12,13,13,561$; denominators in the
last three quadratic formulas are $2,6,10$. Rounding the two roots does
not merge them into one task; each original subpart is one task.

## 1006: a specified root

$a$ is real. Substituting $x=1$ in $ax^2-3x-5$ gives $a-8$,
so $a=8$ is necessary and sufficient. At that value
$8x^2-3x-5=(x-1)(8x+5)$; the roots are $1$ and $-5/8$.

The natural-degree case $a=0$ is the linear equation $-3x-5=0$,
whose only root is $-5/3$, not $1$. No division by an unrestricted
parameter is used.

## 1007: a root equal to one, including degenerations

For every real $a,c$,
$ax^2-(a+c)x+c=(x-1)(ax-c)$, and evaluation at $1$ gives
$a-(a+c)+c=0$.

- $a\ne0$: roots $1,c/a$; they coincide exactly when $c=a$.
- $a=0,c\ne0$: the equation is $-c(x-1)=0$, with only root $1$.
- $a=c=0$: it is the identity $0=0$; every real number is a root,
  including $1$.

Thus the stated claim is true even when the polynomial is not quadratic.

## 1008: coefficient relationships

### а: known root $-1$

For real coefficients, substitution gives $a-b+c=0$, equivalently
$b=a+c$. Conversely that relation factors the polynomial as
$(x+1)(ax+c)$, so it is sufficient.

- $a\ne0$: roots $-1,-c/a$; double root iff $c=a$.
- $a=0,c\ne0$: $b=c$, and the linear equation has only root $-1$.
- $a=c=0$: the relation forces $b=0$, giving all real solutions.

### б: known root $c/a$, with $c\ne0$

The source explicitly requires $c\ne0$; the expression $c/a$ also
requires $a\ne0$. Under both restrictions,

$a(c/a)^2+b(c/a)+c=c(a+b+c)/a$.

Therefore $a+b+c=0$, equivalently $b=-(a+c)$, is necessary and
sufficient. The resulting polynomial is $(x-1)(ax-c)$, with roots
$1,c/a$, repeated exactly when $c=a$.

There is no admitted $a=0$ linear branch here: the specified root would
be undefined. There is no admitted $c=0$ branch either. If that latter
restriction were dropped, $0$ would be a root independently of $b$,
so cancellation of $c$ would no longer justify the relationship.

## 1009: unique roots and the unresolved convention

$a$ is real. A genuine quadratic has a single distinct real root iff
its discriminant is zero, giving the following results:

| Part | Discriminant | Quadratic-only parameter set | Corresponding root |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| а | $784-16a$ | $\{49\}$ | $-7/2$ |
| б | $a^2-1600$ | $\{-40,40\}$ | $a=-40:4/5$; $a=40:-4/5$ |
| в | $784-16a$, with $a\ne0$ | $\{49\}$ | $-2/7$ |
| г | $a^2-456$ | $\{-2\sqrt{114},2\sqrt{114}\}$ | $x=-a/2$ |

For parts а, б, г the leading coefficients are fixed and nonzero, so
the literal "exactly one distinct real solution" interpretation gives
the same sets.

For part в, $a=0$ instead gives $28x+4=0$, whose unique real solution
is $-1/7$. Hence its literal parameter set is $\{0,49\}$, whereas
its quadratic-only set is $\{49\}$.

The full degree-aware classification for part в is:
$a=0$ linear/one solution; $a=49$ quadratic/one solution;
$a<49,a\ne0$ quadratic/two solutions; $a>49$ quadratic/no real solutions.
The displayed source does not explicitly require $a\ne0$. Its intended
convention is not guessed. This ambiguity is retained, not silently resolved.

## 1010: inverse-root correspondence

Let $f(t)=at^2+bt+c$ and $g(x)=cx^2+bx+a$, where
$a\ne0,c\ne0$ and $b$ is real. Both are genuine quadratics.
Since $f(0)=c\ne0$ and $g(0)=a\ne0$, neither has a zero root.

For $x\ne0$, $g(x)=x^2f(1/x)$. Therefore a root $r$ of $f$
gives the root $1/r$ of $g$, and a root $s$ of $g$ gives the
root $1/s$ of $f$. Reciprocity is its own inverse, so this is
a bijection of real root sets, not only a one-way inclusion.

Both discriminants equal $b^2-4ac$. If negative, both real root sets
are empty; if zero, their repeated roots are reciprocal; if positive,
their two distinct roots are paired reciprocally. Multiplicity is
also preserved: if $f(t)=a(t-r_1)(t-r_2)$, then
$g(x)=a(1-r_1x)(1-r_2x)=c(x-1/r_1)(x-1/r_2)$,
using $r_1r_2=c/a$. This factorization applies to the repeated
real-root case as well.

Neither nonzero-coefficient restriction may be removed: zero roots
or a lower-degree polynomial would invalidate the asserted reciprocal
quadratic correspondence.

